  "narration": {
    "title": "The Award Boards: Two Runaways, Three Real Fights",
    "intro": "Late August is when the awards talk stops being idle. By our 0-100 Scores, two names have pulled clear \u2014 Yordan Alvarez in the AL and Jacob Misiorowski for the NL Cy Young \u2014 while the other four races are genuine scraps. Here's who's actually leading, and by how much.",
    "mvp_lead": "Alvarez owns the AL at 85.2, and it isn't close \u2014 his 1.051 OPS is a full tier above the field, with 36 homers and a .322 average to match; Ben Rice (77.5) and Junior Caminero (76.7) are fighting over second. The NL is the tight one: James Wood leads at 78.2 on a .928 OPS for Washington, but Pete Crow-Armstrong is right on him at 77.5, and unlike Wood's cold stretch, Crow-Armstrong is still humming at a .909 clip over his last 30 days.",
    "cy_lead": "Misiorowski is the runaway of the whole edition \u2014 an 89.3 Score built on a 1.68 ERA and a 0.76 WHIP, better than Chris Sale (80.8) can reach even with his 2.20 ERA. The AL is far friendlier: Cam Schlittler paces it at 80.7 with 193 strikeouts, but Dylan Cease (72.9) and Drew Rasmussen (72.5) are all but tied a step behind.",
    "roy_lead": "Munetaka Murakami tops the AL rookies at 72.0, his 29 homers doing the heavy lifting even as the average sits at .219, with Cleveland's Parker Messick (70.6) and his 2.50 ERA close behind. In the NL, Sal Stewart leads at 70.9 \u2014 a rookie with 100 RBI and 28 homers for Cincinnati, and the clearest of the three rookie front-runners."
  }
